---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="How hints work" description="Morningwit hints open in three layers so you can take only as much help as you want. This page explains the layers and the promises behind them.">
	<div class="wrap-narrow prose">
		<h1>How hints work</h1>
		<p>Many hint pages show the answer as soon as you land on them. Here, everything starts covered and you choose how far to go.</p>

		<h2>Three layers</h2>
		<ol>
			<li>Where to look. A direction only: a row, a region, a corner of the board. No answer, no rule.</li>
			<li>What to think. The idea that unlocks the next move, and the trap that catches people at this point.</li>
			<li>The answer for one cell. One square, not the whole board. You still finish it yourself.</li>
		</ol>
		<p>A layer stays shut until you open the one before it. Opening a layer counts as a hint used, and your solve time shows the count next to it, so you always know what you leaned on.</p>

		<h2>What you can count on</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>Nothing is visible until you tap it.</li>
			<li>Hints are worked out from your board as it is now, not from a fixed script. If you have made a mistake, the hint tells you that first.</li>
			<li>We explain the reasoning, not just the result, so the next puzzle goes faster.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>Hints for other daily puzzles</h2>
		<p>
			When we cover a well-known daily puzzle from another publisher, the page follows the same three layers and describes the puzzle in our own words.
			We do not copy the publisher's puzzle content, and we are not connected to them in any way.
		</p>
		<div class="note">
			Morningwit is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or connected to The New York Times. Game names belong to their owners.
		</div>
	</div>
</Layout>
